---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const b = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const faqs = [
  ['Why do firms look for a Docyt alternative?', 'Docyt is an AI-native accounting ERP built for multi-location hospitality and retail, from $299 per property per month. Firms look elsewhere when clients are not hotels or multi-unit retail, must keep QuickBooks Online or Xero, or are too small for per-property pricing.'],
  ['Which Docyt alternative keeps QuickBooks Online or Xero as the books?', 'Booke AI works inside existing QBO or Xero files (Business $129/business/month), Xenett reviews QBO or Xero financials before sign-off, and Truewind runs the close on top of Sage Intacct or QuickBooks Online.'],
  ['What is the cheapest alternative to Docyt?', 'Xenett lists AI Financials Review at $7.50/client/month and Workflow at $10/client/month (USD), with a 14-day free trial. At 10 clients that is $75 to $100 a month, against $2,990 for 10 single-location Docyt properties at list price.'],
  ['When should a firm stay on Docyt?', 'Stay when clients are hotels, restaurants, or multi-location retail or franchise businesses with PMS or POS revenue to post nightly, and you want one ERP that replaces the ledger with continuous reconciliation and a zero-day close.'],
];
const faqLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map(([q, a]) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a } })),
});
---
<Base
  title="Docyt alternatives for bookkeeping firms: AI close without per-property ERP"
  description="Options beyond Docyt’s hospitality/retail ERP for multi-location books: Digits, Truewind, Booke AI and Xenett, with pricing verified Oct 2026 and when each fits better."
>

  <h1>Docyt alternatives</h1>
  <p>Docyt is an AI-native accounting ERP aimed at multi-location hospitality and retail: nightly PMS/POS revenue posting, continuous reconciliation, and a zero-day close that replaces the ledger. Accounting Suite starts at $299 per property per month with unlimited users; catch-up is $200 per month caught up. Firms look elsewhere when clients are not hotels or multi-unit retail, when they must keep QuickBooks Online or Xero as the books, when per-property pricing is too heavy for small SMB clients, or when they want a published per-client meter instead of a property suite. These are the closest options in our directory, not a full market scan.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Alternative</th>
        <th>Best fit vs Docyt</th>
        <th>Pricing (verified Oct 2026)</th>
        <th>Trade-off</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><a href={`${b}/tools/digits`}>Digits</a></td>
        <td>US small-business and startup clients that can move onto an AI-native ledger with 24/7 bookkeeping and Agentic Close</td>
        <td>Per client: Essentials $65/mo, Core $100/mo, Pro $250/mo (US only; no seat fees)</td>
        <td>Also replaces the ledger, but for generic SMB/startup books — not hospitality PMS/POS or per-property billing</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><a href={`${b}/tools/truewind`}>Truewind</a></td>
        <td>Close execution on top of Sage Intacct or QuickBooks Online you already keep, with reviewer sign-off and an audit trail</td>
        <td>By quote (demo based on close complexity and volume)</td>
        <td>Keeps the existing ledger instead of replacing it; no published price card; not built around hotels or per-property suites</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><a href={`${b}/tools/booke-ai`}>Booke AI</a></td>
        <td>Daily AI categorization, OCR document matching and exception review inside existing QBO or Xero files</td>
        <td>Business $129/business/mo; firm/white-label by quote</td>
        <td>Layer on the client’s ledger, not a continuous multi-entity ERP or hospitality revenue posting</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><a href={`${b}/tools/xenett`}>Xenett</a></td>
        <td>Cheap per-client AI review of the P&amp;L and Balance Sheet on QBO or Xero before sign-off</td>
        <td>AI Financials Review $7.50/client/mo; Workflow $10/client/mo (USD); 14-day free trial</td>
        <td>Flags issues and tracks tasks; does not run continuous recon, replace the ledger, or post PMS/POS revenue</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Cost at 10 properties / clients (list prices, monthly)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Docyt: from $299 per property, so 10 single-location properties start at $2,990 (portfolio pricing can scale down; Enterprise custom).</li>
    <li>Digits: 10 × $65 = $650 (Essentials) to 10 × $100 = $1,000 (Core); Pro (listed as coming soon) would be 10 × $250 = $2,500.</li>
    <li>Truewind: not published.</li>
    <li>Booke AI: 10 × $129 = $1,290 (Business); firm plan custom.</li>
    <li>Xenett: 10 × $7.50 = $75 (review only) or 10 × $10 = $100 (Workflow).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to stay on Docyt</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Clients are hotels, restaurants, or multi-location retail/franchise with PMS or POS revenue to post nightly and USALI-style operating statements.</li>
    <li>You want one AI-native ERP that replaces the ledger, with continuous bank/card recon and a zero-day close, priced per property with unlimited users.</li>
    <li>Portfolio migration, catch-up accounting, and dedicated hospitality onboarding matter more than keeping QBO/Xero or a cheap per-client review layer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  {faqs.map(([q, a]) => (<><h3>{q}</h3><p>{a}</p></>))}
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={faqLd} />

  <p>
    <a href={`${b}/tools/docyt`}>Docyt</a>
    · <a href={`${b}/compare/docyt-vs-digits-vs-truewind`}>Docyt vs Digits vs Truewind</a>
    · <a href={`${b}/compare/docyt-vs-xenett`}>Docyt vs Xenett</a>
    · <a href={`${b}/compare/digits-alternatives`}>Digits alternatives</a>
    · <a href={`${b}/category/month-end-close`}>All month-end close tools</a>
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem; }
  th, td { border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 0.5rem 0.6rem; text-align: left; vertical-align: top; }
  th { background: #f6f6f6; }
</style>
